{"id": "34596", "revid": "240096", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=34596", "title": "Sumpat", "text": "Sumpat is the area along Sumpat Bay, in the north of Bintan island in Indonesia, between Bintan Resorts and Trikora.\nUnderstand.\nThe area consists of a few fishing villages along the beaches around the Sumpat river (\"Sungai Sumpat\"), and with the small Sumpat island (\"Pulau Sumpat\") just off the coast. Administratively, the area is in the villages (\"desa\") of Berakit and Pengudang in the district of Teluk Sebong.\nGet in.\nFor detailed information on how to travel to and from Bintan island see the main Bintan#Get in section. A new international ferry terminal is planned in nearby Berakit and should be open in 2011/12.\nGet around.\nSumpat is located about half way (30 km) between Bintan Resorts and Tanjung Pinang.\nThe best way to reach it from the ferry terminal is by taxi or car / motorbike rental.\nOn the main road from Tanjung Pinang to Tanjung Uban turn at 46 km to take the local road to Pengudang. Alternatively Sumpat can also be reached from Tanjung Pinang via the road from Trikora beach going towards Berakit.\nSee.\nThe main attractions are the unspoilt, white sandy beaches that stretch on for several kilometers.\nThe area is also a popular fishing destination for local people. Kelong (floating fisherman houses) are a popular sight off the coast.\nDo.\nThe area is still widely undeveloped. But with more and more resorts and guest houses\ntaking up the east coast along Trikora, the beaches around Sumpat are becoming an insider's tip\nfor local tourists.\nRent a sampan to go Sumpat island or walk there during low tide.\nSee the mangroves along Sungai Sumpat river.\nBuy.\nThere are only a few shops catering to the needs of the local residents, selling food and daily supplies.\nEat.\nTry the freshly caught seafood from the local fishing villages. During low tide you may see the women bringing back crab.\nSleep.\nMid-range.\nPutri Pandan Resort. Jalan Hamidi, Desa Pengudang, Tel. +62-813-7287-3630.\nBoutique resort located on a private beach. The roof of each bungalow is modelled after a different traditional Indonesian house. Large rooms with private bathroom and hot water shower. English (German, Japanese) speaking staff. Beach front wooden bungalows starting from S$ 30 per double room.\nThe restaurant at the resort is also open to outside visitors.\nGo next.\nTransportation back to the ferry terminal can be arranged by the resorts."}
{"id": "34597", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=34597", "title": "Sumqayit", "text": "Sumqayit, or Sumgayit (\"Sumqay\u0131t\") is the third-largest city in Azerbaijan.\nUnderstand.\nSumqayit was one of the biggest industrial cities of its time during the Soviet Union era. There were nearly 90 heavy-to-medium industrial plants but nowadays only 5 or 6 of them remain. The city was planned and built in 1950s so that the industrial zone was 3-4 km away from residential areas in an unsuccessful attempt to minimize the pollution effects on people. This is why the coast line of the city consists of so much green area.\nGet in.\nBy marshrutka.\nIt is around 45 km (27 miles) from Baku, or a 1-hr drive away by bus.\nThere are direct marshrutkas from Ismailli for 4.20 manat. \nBy taxi.\nSince gas prices are as low as water prices in Azerbaijan, only an estimated cost of 10 manat should be expected for a ride from Baku to Sumqayit by taxi. You can bargain though, and the taxi drives can go as low as 7 manat\u2014some travellers even reported 3.50 manat.\nBy car.\nIt is around 45 km (30-40 min) from Baku center Targovi to Sumqayit coast (G\u00f6yercin).\nDo.\nAlso, see Baku Region for nearby sights and dos.\nDrink.\nFor an authentic experience, there are a few board game caf\u00e9s in town, where you can drink tea and watch the locals (mostly elder men) play games. Ask a local if you cannot seem to find them.\nSleep.\nSome apartments are for rent in Sumgayit."}
{"id": "34598", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=34598", "title": "Sumter", "text": "Sumter is a city in Santee Cooper Country, South Carolina.\nUnderstand.\nFrom the world famous Swan Lake Iris Garden to the historic Sumter Opera House, Sumter boasts of unique sights and a colorful lifestyle. A rather perfect blend of antebellum era and 21st century, you'll find plantation-style homes juxtaposed with entities like Shaw Air Force Base. Sports and cultural events are also a draw, and the city's location within a couple of hours from the mountains and sea, makes its motto \"In the Middle of Everything\u201d fairly correct."}
